# A rework loop bounded by the tracker's own count of the times it has handed
# an item back (DR-0088). Nothing in the rule body advances the count: the
# provider does, on every `release`, so there is no bookkeeping to forget.
#
# The ring carries no fact at all — its only edge is `review --tracker:backlog-->
# review` — which is the case a measure could not reach until `WorkItem.releases`
# existed. Drop the `where` clause and `whip check` warns that nothing bounds
# the loop; change `release` to `file` and the warning comes back for a subtler
# reason, because a NEW item starts its count at zero.
@service
workflow MeasuredReleaseRing
use std.tracker

tracker backlog { provider builtin }

agent reviewer { provider fixture  profile "repo-writer"  capacity 1 }

# The loop. Three turns per item, counted by the tracker.
rule review
  when backlog has ready issue as issue where issue.releases < 3
=> {
  claim issue as claimed

  after claimed completes {
    tell reviewer "review {{ issue.title }}" as turn

    after turn completes {
      release issue
    }
  }
}

# The bound stops the rule above from MATCHING, which would leave an exhausted
# item sitting ready and unclaimed forever. The second rule is what makes the
# ring settle rather than merely stop turning.
rule escalate
  when backlog has ready issue as issue where issue.releases >= 3
=> {
  claim issue as held

  after held completes {
    finish issue { summary "three reviews without a verdict; escalated" }
  }
}
